/// Generalized holes as stacks of coaxial features (chamfers, counterbores,
/// blind and through bores, drive recesses), returned as void solids to
/// subtract.

// Drill: generalized holes
//
// A hole is a stack of coaxial features on the +Z axis, its mouth at a given
// plane, cut downward. Every entry point returns a VOID SOLID — the material
// to remove — so the caller subtracts it:
//
//   max(block, -drill.hole([drill.chamfer(12.0, 0.5), drill.blind(12.0, 30.0)], 0))
//
// the same convention fastener's receiving features use.
//
// Depths are measured from the MOUTH, the way a drawing callout dimensions
// them: a counterbore 6.5 deep is 6.5 from the face, not 6.5 past whatever is
// above it. So the list order does not change the geometry, and the numbers in
// it are the numbers on the print.
//
// NO SURFACE OF THE VOID MAY LIE IN ANY FACE IT OPENS ONTO. Two coincident
// surfaces cannot be resolved: such a hole evaluates correctly at every point
// you would think to sample, passes its tests, and renders as no hole at all.
// So every feature here is bounded BELOW, by its own depth, and open ABOVE —
// the part's own face is what bounds it, exactly as gears lets an unbounded
// cylinderZ be its bore, and which is also why an omitted depth means THRU.
//
// That is about the EXIT face as much as the entry one: a groove stopped at
// exactly the far side of the part leaves an infinitely thin web there, which
// is the same defect upside down.
//
// So features whose span is their own — a spline, a keyway — come in three
// forms: through (no bounds at all), open-topped down to a toDepth, and a
// *Between bounded at both ends. The last is for a groove that genuinely lies
// inside the bore; giving it a bound on either face puts a surface back in it.
// And chamferAt / countersinkAt stop on a plane BELOW the mouth, which is
// inside the hole rather than on the part, so they may bound themselves.
//
// fastener's holes are these holes. fastener.shcsHole, countersunkHole,
// clearanceHole and tappedHole are drill.hole stacks sized by SCREW, from ISO
// tables, in the screw's own frame; the features here are sized by DIMENSION.
// Reach for fastener when a fastener goes in the hole and for drill when a
// drawing gives you diameters — it is the same hole either way.
//
// Doc convention follows builtin/base.gsl: `///` lines are public docs.

// Each feature is a FACTORY: called with the feature's own dimensions, it
// returns a closure that takes the mouth plane. That is what lets hole() place
// a whole stack at once, and what will let the shaft hand its end holes a
// plane its own fold computed. It also means the accumulator only ever meets
// min and max — never remap, which is a silent no-op on a shape that arrives
// as a parameter.

// =========================================================
// Point Angles
// =========================================================

/// The jobber-drill point: 118 degrees included. The general-purpose grind,
/// and what blind() uses when you do not say.

pointAngle118 = radians(118.0);
/// The split point: 135 degrees included. Stainless, hardened, and anything
/// that wants a self-centering start.
pointAngle135 = radians(135.0);
/// The 60-degree included angle of an ANSI B94.11 center, and of the pilot
/// countersink centerHole() cuts.
centerAngle60 = radians(60.0);

// =========================================================
// Entry Points
// =========================================================

/// The void solid of a hole whose mouth is the plane z = `zTop`, cut
/// downward. `features` is a list of feature closures from this package —
/// drill.thru, drill.bore, drill.countersink and the rest. Every depth in
/// them is measured from the mouth, so the list reads like the lines of a
/// drawing callout and its order does not matter.
///
/// A void solid: subtract it with max(block, -drill.hole([...], 0)). Place it on
/// a face with remap() and drill.axisAt().
hole(features, zTop) {
  // Alias into a local: a fold callback captures enclosing locals, which is
  // the same reason catalog's table lookups alias their key (`k = nominal;`).
  mouth = zTop;
  // The seed is written out rather than named. This body is CLONED INTO THE
  // CALLER'S FILE — that is where the features' own arguments live — so every
  // name in it has to resolve there. builtin is dot-imported everywhere, so
  // min, max and p are fine; a name of this package's would not be. Keep this
  // function self-contained, and put anything that needs bore's own scope
  // inside a feature closure, whose body stays here.
  //
  // The stack is NOT clipped at the mouth. A void that stopped exactly at the
  // face would put its top surface on that face, and a surface coincident with
  // another cannot be resolved — the hole evaluates correctly at every point
  // and still renders as no hole at all. Bodies run past the mouth and the
  // part's own face bounds them, which is what gears already does for its
  // bore and what makes an omitted depth mean THRU. Only the cones are
  // bounded, since those would otherwise widen without limit.
  return reduce((acc, f) { return min(acc, f(mouth)); }, features, nothing);
}

/// The same stack with its mouth at z = 0.
hole(features) {
  return hole(features, 0.0);
}

// =========================================================
// Internal Geometry
// =========================================================

// A downward-narrowing cone of large diameter `dia` at z = plane, opening
// upward at `included`. Below its own apex it contributes nothing, so a
// lead-in needs no depth.
//
// Above `plane` it is capped by its rim's CYLINDER, `dia` across, not by a
// plane. A cone cut off on the face would put its rim annulus exactly on that
// face, and no surface of the void may be coincident with the face it opens
// onto; a cone left open widens without limit and cuts through whatever stands
// above the face. The cylinder meets the face only in the rim circle, and above
// the mouth it is the side of whatever seats in the cone, a flat head's or a
// center's, so the part bounds it as it bounds the bodies.
coneVoid(plane, dia, included) {
  half = included / 2.0;
  apex = plane - (dia / 2.0) / tan(half);
  return max(cone([0.0, 0.0, apex], vector.dirZ, half), cylinderZ(vector.zero, dia / 2.0));
}

// coneVoid cut off at its own plane, for a lead-in seated BELOW the mouth,
// where the plane it stops on is inside the hole rather than on the part's
// face. Never use this at the mouth.
coneVoidAt(plane, dia, included) {
  return max(coneVoid(plane, dia, included), p.z - plane);
}

// A drilled hole: the full-diameter body, floored by the conical point the
// drill leaves.
//
// The point REPLACES the floor rather than being unioned under one. A flat cap
// at the shoulder with a cone stuck beneath it is two surfaces where the tool
// leaves one, and they meet in a seam the geometry never had: on the axis at
// the shoulder the capped body reads 0 and the capped cone reads 0, so the
// field claims a surface 3.6 mm above the tip of a 12 mm drill. Bounding the
// cylinder BY the cone instead is one max, one surface, and a true distance
// everywhere down the hole.
drillVoid(zTop, dia, depth, included) {
  half = included / 2.0;
  apex = (zTop - depth) - (dia / 2.0) / tan(half);
  return max(cylinderZ(vector.zero, dia / 2.0),
             cone([0.0, 0.0, apex], vector.dirZ, half));
}

/// A HEXAGONAL section in XY, `acrossFlats` wide, flats facing +-x: every hex
/// head, nut, socket and bar in the mechanical packages is this one field.
/// Unit-gradient (a max of six half-planes via abs).
hexXY(acrossFlats) {
  h = acrossFlats / 2.0;
  return max(abs(p.x) - h,
             abs(0.5 * p.x + 0.8660254 * p.y) - h,
             abs(0.5 * p.x - 0.8660254 * p.y) - h);
}

/// A SQUARE section in XY, `acrossFlats` wide: the square head's and the
/// Robertson's.
squareXY(acrossFlats) {
  h = acrossFlats / 2.0;
  return max(abs(p.x) - h, abs(p.y) - h);
}

// squareTaperVoid: a square socket whose four walls lean inward as they go
// down. A taper grips a tapered bit hard enough to hold the screw on the end of
// the driver and still lets it go under a straight pull, which is how the
// Robertson is usually described — but the patent's walls are parallel and its
// taper is in the pyramid at the bottom, and a modern recess needs no wall taper
// to hold a bit. robertsonVoid below is that form; this one stays for a socket
// that really is tapered.
//
// One plane per wall, by the same algebra crossWing uses: at depth d the half
// width is halfAF - d*tan(taper), and multiplying through by cos(taper) gives a
// gradient of magnitude 1 without any scale factor.
squareTaperVoid(zTop, halfAF, taper, depth) {
  ct = cos(taper);
  st = sin(taper);
  return max(abs(p.x) * ct + (zTop - p.z) * st - halfAF * ct,
             abs(p.y) * ct + (zTop - p.z) * st - halfAF * ct,
             (zTop - depth) - p.z);
}

// robertsonVoid: the square recess as P.L. Robertson patented it (US 975,285,
// 1909, https://patents.google.com/patent/US975285A/en) — four PARALLEL walls `halfAF` from the axis, and the taper at the
// BOTTOM: a chamfer of `leg` at 45 degrees running out from the floor to the
// walls. `depth` is to the floor, the deepest point.
//
// The chamfer is the whole range of bottoms in one number. A leg of zero is a
// flat-bottomed square socket; a leg of halfAF meets itself on the axis and is
// the patent's four-sided pyramid, the point the bit's own tip was ground to.
// Each chamfer is one plane per wall, the same 45-degree plane chamferSection
// writes, so the void stays a max of half-spaces and unit-gradient by
// construction.
robertsonVoid(zTop, halfAF, depth, leg) {
  bottom = zTop - depth;
  return max(abs(p.x) - halfAF, abs(p.y) - halfAF, bottom - p.z,
             ((abs(p.x) - halfAF) + (bottom - p.z) + leg) * 0.70710678,
             ((abs(p.y) - halfAF) + (bottom - p.z) + leg) * 0.70710678);
}

// torxXY: the hexalobular section of ISO 10664 (2014,
// https://cdn.standards.iteh.ai/samples/63207/f79670cf0a214881aa40b4822580cce3/ISO-10664-2014.pdf)
// — a disc of diameter `major`
// with six circular bites taken out of its rim, at `filletR`, reaching in to
// diameter `minor`.
//
// BITES, NOT LOBES, and that is the whole trick. Six lobe circles unioned
// together leave a sharp reentrant corner where each pair crosses, and the
// standard has a concave ARC there — the fillet is most of why a Torx bit does
// not split the recess, since a reentrant corner is a crack waiting to start.
// Subtracting circles from a disc gives convex lobe arcs off the disc and
// concave fillet arcs off the bites, which is the profile as drawn, and it is
// exact: one max and one min of circles, no polar radius function to bound and
// no scale factor to apologize for.
//
// The bites sit at minor/2 + filletR, so they reach exactly to the minor
// diameter. Bigger fillets make narrower lobes.
torxXY(major, minor, filletR) {
  bite = length([p.x - (minor / 2.0 + filletR), p.y]) - filletR;
  bites = remap(bite, map.patternPolarXY(6));
  return max(length(p.xy) - major / 2.0, -bites);
}

// torxXY at the fillet radius the drawn proportion wants: 0.4 of the lobe
// depth, which leaves the lobes a little wider than the gaps between them, as
// ISO 10664 draws them.
torxXY(major, minor) {
  return torxXY(major, minor, 0.4 * (major - minor));
}

// capsuleXY: the 2D obround of diameter `dia` whose end centers are `centers`
// apart, the long axis at `ang` radians from +X.
capsuleXY(dia, centers, ang) {
  ca = cos(ang);
  sa = sin(ang);
  qx = p.x * ca + p.y * sa;
  qy = -p.x * sa + p.y * ca;
  ex = max(abs(qx) - centers / 2.0, 0.0);
  return length([ex, qy]) - dia / 2.0;
}

// crossWing: one tapered wing of a cross recess — an infinite slab about the
// plane `across` = 0, half `halfW` wide at the recess face `zTop` and narrowing
// by `flank` radians as it goes down.
//
// The taper is what a cross recess IS, and it is one plane per side rather than
// a shape: at depth d below the face the half width is halfW - d*tan(flank),
// and multiplying through by cos(flank) turns that into
//
//   |across| * cos(flank) + (zTop - z) * sin(flank) - halfW * cos(flank)
//
// whose gradient is (cos, sin) in magnitude 1, so the wing is a unit-gradient
// field by construction and not by scaling. Passing `across` as p.x or p.y is
// the whole of the four-fold symmetry: two slabs min-ed together are a cross.
crossWing(across, halfW, flank, zTop) {
  cf = cos(flank);
  return abs(across) * cf + (zTop - p.z) * sin(flank) - halfW * cf;
}

// crossVoid: the cruciform recess itself. Four wings of half-width `halfW`
// tapering at `flank`, reaching out to radius `outerR`, cut `depth` below the
// face. Unbounded ABOVE, like every other void here, so the head's own top
// face bounds it and no two surfaces land on one plane.
crossVoid(zTop, halfW, outerR, depth, flank) {
  arms = min(crossWing(p.y, halfW, flank, zTop),
             crossWing(p.x, halfW, flank, zTop));
  return max(arms, length(p.xy) - outerR, (zTop - depth) - p.z);
}

// crossRibbedVoid: the same with four secondary ribs at 45 degrees between the
// wings — narrower, shallower and shorter slots, the same construction rotated
// 45 degrees, which in a field is just reading the rotated coordinates.
//
// THIS IS NOT THE ISO POZIDRIV, whatever it looks like at arm's length. ISO
// 4757 draws a type Z rib as a thin V-groove that runs OUT to the recess
// diameter and a little past it — "1 to 1.25 m" tip to tip — not as a slot
// stopping short of the wings; pozidrivVoid below is that form. This one stays
// as a general ribbed cross, with its ribs' reach its own parameter.
crossRibbedVoid(zTop, halfW, outerR, depth, flank, ribHalfW, ribFlank, ribDepth, ribOuterR) {
  arms = min(crossWing(p.y, halfW, flank, zTop),
             crossWing(p.x, halfW, flank, zTop));
  u = (p.x + p.y) * 0.70710678;
  v = (p.x - p.y) * 0.70710678;
  ribs = max(min(crossWing(v, ribHalfW, ribFlank, zTop),
                 crossWing(u, ribHalfW, ribFlank, zTop)),
             length(p.xy) - ribOuterR,
             (zTop - ribDepth) - p.z);
  return max(min(arms, ribs), length(p.xy) - outerR, (zTop - depth) - p.z);
}

// crossRibbedRootVoid: the ribbed cross with the plan corner fillet of
// crossRootVoid. The ribs sit in those corners, so the fillet is applied to the
// WINGS before the ribs join them — round the whole eight-armed union and the
// ribs blend into the wings and stop being ribs.
crossRibbedRootVoid(zTop, halfW, outerR, depth, flank,
                    ribHalfW, ribFlank, ribDepth, ribOuterR, rootR) {
  return crossRibbedRootVoid(zTop, halfW, outerR, depth, flank,
                             ribHalfW, ribFlank, ribDepth, ribOuterR, rootR, 0.0);
}

crossRibbedRootVoid(zTop, halfW, outerR, depth, flank,
                    ribHalfW, ribFlank, ribDepth, ribOuterR, rootR, outerTaper) {
  arms = unionRound(crossWing(p.y, halfW, flank, zTop),
                    crossWing(p.x, halfW, flank, zTop), rootR);
  u = (p.x + p.y) * 0.70710678;
  v = (p.x - p.y) * 0.70710678;
  ribs = max(min(crossWing(v, ribHalfW, ribFlank, zTop),
                 crossWing(u, ribHalfW, ribFlank, zTop)),
             crossOuterVoid(ribOuterR, outerTaper, zTop),
             (zTop - ribDepth) - p.z);
  return max(min(arms, ribs), crossOuterVoid(outerR, outerTaper, zTop),
             (zTop - depth) - p.z);
}

// pozidrivCore: the CORE of an ISO 4757 type Z form with its four VALLEYS —
// a square-ish region whose corners run out along the diagonals. Folded to the
// first quadrant, the valley's apex is on the diagonal, apexR out at height
// zk and leaning out by `lean` going up; its two walls meet the wings' walls
// at 46 degrees plus `delta` of metal, so each is delta - 44 degrees off the
// square. Negative inside. The walls contain the leaning apex line, and the
// division by k makes each a unit-gradient plane.
pozidrivCore(apexR, delta, lean, zk) {
  x = abs(p.x);
  y = abs(p.y);
  a = (apexR + (p.z - zk) * tan(lean)) * 0.70710678;
  eps = delta - 0.76794487;
  ce = cos(eps);
  se = sin(eps);
  tilt = tan(lean) * 0.70710678 * (ce + se);
  k = sqrt(1.0 + tilt * tilt);
  return max((x - a) * ce + (y - a) * se, (y - a) * ce + (x - a) * se) / k;
}

// pozidrivWings: four wings `wing` wide on the axes, walls parallel to it.
pozidrivWings(wing) {
  return min(abs(p.y) - wing / 2.0, abs(p.x) - wing / 2.0);
}

// pozidrivVoid: the ISO 4757 TYPE Z recess. The wings and the core, cut to
// the 26 degree 30 cone that is `m` across at the reference plane `zRef` and
// down to the 28 degree bottom that cone meets at the knee, `knee` across;
// the valleys are `core` apart across the diagonal there. Plus the four
// MARKS: V-grooves `markAngle` across along the diagonals, `markDepth` deep
// where they leave the valleys at the reference plane and rising to nothing
// `markReach` out.
//
// NOT MODELLED: ISO's beta and gamma (ASME's "milling offset" angles), which
// tilt the valley and wing walls a few tenths of a degree; r1 at the mouth
// and r2 on the valley edges.
//
// REFERENCES: ISO 4757:1983, https://cdn.standards.iteh.ai/samples/10742/d878fa0371e041b39de75de4d4426928/ISO-4757-1983.pdf;
// IS 7478:2011 (its verbatim adoption, with the type Z gauge), https://archive.org/details/gov.in.is.7478.2011;
// US 3,237,506, Fig. 3, https://patents.google.com/patent/US3237506A/en.
pozidrivVoid(zRef, m, core, wing, knee, lean, delta, markDepth, markReach, markAngle) {
  we = 0.46251225;
  bot = 0.48869219;
  r = length(p.xy);
  zk = zRef - (m - knee) / 2.0 / tan(we);
  zPoint = zk - (knee / 2.0) * tan(bot);
  body = max(r * cos(we) + (zRef - p.z) * sin(we) - (m / 2.0) * cos(we),
             (zPoint - p.z) * cos(bot) + r * sin(bot),
             min(pozidrivWings(wing), pozidrivCore(core / 2.0, delta, lean, zk)));
  inner = core / 2.0 + (zRef - zk) * tan(lean);
  slope = markDepth / max(markReach - inner, 0.001);
  along = (abs(p.x) + abs(p.y)) * 0.70710678;
  across = abs(abs(p.x) - abs(p.y)) * 0.70710678;
  floorZ = zRef - markDepth + (along - inner) * slope;
  half = markAngle / 2.0;
  mark = (across * cos(half) - (p.z - floorZ) * sin(half))
         / sqrt(1.0 + slope * slope * sin(half) * sin(half));
  return min(body, mark);
}

// pozidrivGaugeSolid: the type Z GAUGE — the point of a Pozidriv screwdriver
// — as a solid: the same wings and core inside the 26 degree 30 cone through
// its knee at zKnee, its end an 18 degree facet cut off `point` below.
pozidrivGaugeSolid(zKnee, zTop, core, wing, knee, point, lean, delta) {
  we = 0.46251225;
  ch = 0.31415927;
  r = length(p.xy);
  return max(r * cos(we) - (p.z - zKnee) * sin(we) - (knee / 2.0) * cos(we),
             (zKnee - p.z) * cos(ch) + (r - knee / 2.0) * sin(ch),
             (zKnee - point) - p.z,
             p.z - zTop,
             min(pozidrivWings(wing), pozidrivCore(core / 2.0, delta, lean, zKnee)));
}

// crossRootVoid: the cruciform with a fillet of radius `rootR` in the corners
// where adjacent wings meet, seen in PLAN.
//
// It is a plan-view radius rather than anything at the mouth. Rounded, the
// arms of a Phillips appear to flow into one another; left sharp, a cross
// reads as four straight slots.
//
// The fillet is a ROUNDED UNION of the two wings, which is the same thing said
// in fields: the wings are unioned to make the cross, the corner in question is
// that union's seam, and rounding a seam is what unionRound is. There is no
// scale factor and no patched gradient — the fillet is a construction.
crossRootVoid(zTop, halfW, outerR, depth, flank, rootR) {
  return crossRootVoid(zTop, halfW, outerR, depth, flank, rootR, 0.0);
}

// crossOuterVoid: the OUTER TRIM of a cross recess — the surface that cuts the
// wings off at the recess diameter — leaning out at `outerTaper` so the recess
// opens toward the face.
//
// A CYLINDER HERE IS THE EASY THING TO WRITE AND IT IS WRONG. The wing ends
// are not vertical walls: the whole recess flares outward as it comes up to
// the face, which is what lets a driver find the recess and seat itself, and
// on a Phillips it is part of why the bit rides where it does. Passing 0 gives
// back the cylinder exactly — cos 0 is 1 and sin 0 is 0 — so nothing that did
// not ask for a taper moves.
//
// Same one-plane algebra as crossWing, with the radius standing in for the
// across-coordinate, so it is unit-gradient off the axis by construction.
crossOuterVoid(outerR, outerTaper, zTop) {
  cf = cos(outerTaper);
  return length(p.xy) * cf + (zTop - p.z) * sin(outerTaper) - outerR * cf;
}

crossRootVoid(zTop, halfW, outerR, depth, flank, rootR, outerTaper) {
  arms = unionRound(crossWing(p.y, halfW, flank, zTop),
                    crossWing(p.x, halfW, flank, zTop), rootR);
  return max(arms, crossOuterVoid(outerR, outerTaper, zTop),
             (zTop - depth) - p.z);
}

// phillipsRibs: the four RIBS of an ISO 4757 type H form, on the diagonals —
// the metal left standing between the wings of a recess, and the flutes cut
// between the blades of its driver. Negative inside a rib.
//
// Each rib is written in the section SQUARE TO ITS RIDGE, which is where ISO
// draws its angles. The ridge points at the axis, b/2 out at height `zb`, and
// leans out by `lean` going up; s is the distance from the ridge along the
// rib's own centre plane, measured square to the ridge, so every face below is
// a plane through a line parallel to the ridge and needs no rotation. Across
// the ridge the rib is a blunt facet at `ridge` radians, `facet` wide, then
// flanks 92 degrees apart: a max of half-planes, unit-gradient by construction.
//
// NO. 0 HAS A RADIUS for a ridge instead of the facets. A `root` above zero
// swaps them for a 92 degree wedge rounded at `root` with its tip on the ridge
// — the wedge's exact distance, so the round is a round and not a corner
// pulled back — and `facet` and `ridge` are then not read.
//
// The 92 degree flanks close on each other outward by 2 degrees, so a wing is
// a hair narrower at its end than at the core; they meet again about fifteen
// recess radii out, far outside any cone that bounds them.
//
// REFERENCES: ISO 4757:1983, the type H figures and Tables 1 and 2,
// https://cdn.standards.iteh.ai/samples/10742/d878fa0371e041b39de75de4d4426928/ISO-4757-1983.pdf;
// FreeCAD Fasteners reads the drawing the same way (makeHCrossRecess),
// https://raw.githubusercontent.com/shaise/FreeCAD_FastenersWB/master/screw_maker.py.
phillipsRibs(core, facet, ridge, lean, root, zb) {
  x = abs(p.x);
  y = abs(p.y);
  u = (x + y) * 0.70710678;
  w = abs(x - y) * 0.70710678;
  s = (u - core / 2.0) * cos(lean) - (p.z - zb) * sin(lean);
  c = 0.69465837;
  sn = 0.71933980;
  half = ridge / 2.0;
  sFacet = (facet / 2.0) / tan(max(half, 0.1));
  faceted = max(w * cos(half) - s * sin(half),
                (w - facet / 2.0) * c - (s - sFacet) * sn);
  qs = s - root;
  t = max(qs * c + w * sn, 0.0);
  rounded = sign(w * c - qs * sn) * length([qs - t * c, w - t * sn]) - root;
  return root > 0.0 ? rounded : faceted;
}

// phillipsVoid: the ISO 4757 TYPE H recess — the ribs cut away from a cone.
// The cone is `m` across at the reference plane `zRef` and leans out at 26
// degrees 30 from the axis; it meets the bottom, a 28 degree cone to a point,
// at diameter `g`; the ribs' ridges are `core` apart where they meet that
// bottom. Unbounded above: the cone keeps opening, and the head bounds it.
phillipsVoid(zRef, m, core, facet, g, ridge, lean, root) {
  we = 0.46251225;
  bot = 0.48869219;
  r = length(p.xy);
  zg = zRef - (m - g) / 2.0 / tan(we);
  zPoint = zg - (g / 2.0) * tan(bot);
  zb = zg - ((g - core) / 2.0) * tan(bot);
  return max(r * cos(we) + (zRef - p.z) * sin(we) - (m / 2.0) * cos(we),
             (zPoint - p.z) * cos(bot) + r * sin(bot),
             neg(phillipsRibs(core, facet, ridge, lean, root, zb)));
}

// splineVoid: `count` straight-sided grooves out to `major`, each `width`
// across, unbounded along z. patternPolarXY passes p.z through, so bounding
// z outside the remap is the same as bounding it inside and keeps the two
// spline forms sharing one body.
splineVoid(count, major, width) {
  slot = max(abs(p.y) - width / 2.0, length(p.xy) - major / 2.0);
  return remap(slot, map.patternPolarXY(count));
}

// keywayVoid: one slot on +x, `width` across, cut `depth` radially outward
// from a bore of `boreDia`, unbounded along z.
keywayVoid(width, depth, boreDia) {
  return max(abs(p.y) - width / 2.0,
             length(p.xy) - (boreDia / 2.0 + depth),
             neg(p.x));
}

// A prismatic section cut to `depth` below the mouth. The section is already
// a 2D field; only the floor has to be added, since the mouth is clipped once
// by hole() and everything above a feature was cut wider.
prismVoid(zTop, section, depth) {
  return max(section, (zTop - depth) - p.z);
}

// A 45-degree lead-in that FOLLOWS a section rather than assuming a circle:
// the section grown by `leg` at `plane`, back to the section itself one leg
// below, and narrowing to nothing under that. Growing a 2D distance field is
// subtracting from it, so the chamfer of any section is the section minus a
// ramp in z — and the 0.7071 is the same normalization coneVoid gets from
// cone() for free.
//
// Capped above by its rim, the section grown by `leg`, as coneVoid is by its
// rim's cylinder and for the same reason: open, it would widen without limit
// through whatever stands above the face. `section` is a 2D
// FIELD, a plain parameter the caller has already evaluated; a feature taking
// a section as a FUNCTION would be the general chamfer, and that is still the
// captured-function case the closure pass bakes rather than lifts.
chamferSection(plane, section, leg) {
  return max((section - leg - (p.z - plane)) * 0.7071, section - leg);
}

// =========================================================
// Stacked Features
// =========================================================

/// A drilled hole clean through the part: diameter `dia`, no floor. The THRU
/// of a drawing callout — the part's own faces bound it, and there is no
/// drill point in it because the drill went out the far side.
thru(dia) {
  cut(zTop) {
    return cylinderZ(vector.zero, dia / 2.0);
  }
  return cut;
}

/// A BLIND drilled hole of diameter `dia`, `depth` deep to the full-diameter
/// shoulder — the depth a drawing dimensions. Below it sits the 118-degree
/// point the drill leaves, which is never dimensioned: for a 6 mm drill it
/// adds 1.80.
blind(dia, depth) {
  cut(zTop) {
    return drillVoid(zTop, dia, depth, pointAngle118);
  }
  return cut;
}

/// A drilled hole with the point angle given, `included` in radians —
/// drill.pointAngle118 or drill.pointAngle135. `depth` still reaches the
/// full-diameter shoulder, not the tip.
blind(dia, depth, included) {
  cut(zTop) {
    return drillVoid(zTop, dia, depth, included);
  }
  return cut;
}

/// A BORED hole of diameter `dia`, `depth` deep, flat bottomed: boring is the
/// operation that opens an existing hole to size and concentricity, so this is
/// also the counterbore, the end-milled pocket and the reamed flat. The
/// counterbore line of a callout.
bore(dia, depth) {
  cut(zTop) {
    return max(cylinderZ(vector.zero, dia / 2.0), (zTop - depth) - p.z);
  }
  return cut;
}

/// A 45-degree chamfer whose large diameter is `dia` at the mouth. It narrows
/// to nothing, so whatever follows it cuts it off: list it with the bore it
/// leads into.
chamfer(dia) {
  cut(zTop) {
    return coneVoid(zTop, dia, radians(90.0));
  }
  return cut;
}

/// A 45-degree chamfer of leg `leg` on a bore of diameter `dia` — the drawing
/// note "0.5 x 45 CHAMFER" on a 6 mm hole is drill.chamfer(6.0, 0.5).
chamfer(dia, leg) {
  cut(zTop) {
    return coneVoid(zTop, dia + 2.0 * leg, radians(90.0));
  }
  return cut;
}

/// A 45-degree chamfer of leg `leg` on a bore of diameter `dia`, its large
/// diameter `atDepth` below the mouth rather than at it — the lead-in on a
/// bore that starts at the floor of a counterbore, which a drawing dimensions
/// on that bore and not on the face.
///
/// The general form of this is a combinator, at(depth, feature), which would
/// shift ANY feature down. It cannot be written yet: it would have to capture
/// a function, and a captured function is the one thing the closure pass still
/// bakes rather than lifts.
chamferAt(dia, leg, atDepth) {
  cut(zTop) {
    return coneVoidAt(zTop - atDepth, dia + 2.0 * leg, radians(90.0));
  }
  return cut;
}

/// A countersink of large diameter `dia` at `included` angle in radians — the
/// drawing spells it "CSK DIA 13 X 90", diameter and angle, never a depth. Use
/// radians(90) for an ISO 10642 flat head, radians(82) for the inch series.
/// To break its own outer edge, list a wider chamfer with it; that needs no
/// new function, which is the point of a feature list.
///
/// Above the mouth the void is the rim's cylinder, `dia` across: the side of
/// the head that seats in it. The cone stops widening there, so it cuts no
/// wider than the head through anything that stands above the face.
countersink(dia, included) {
  cut(zTop) {
    return coneVoid(zTop, dia, included);
  }
  return cut;
}

/// A countersink whose rim sits `atDepth` below the mouth rather than at it.
countersinkAt(dia, included, atDepth) {
  cut(zTop) {
    return coneVoidAt(zTop - atDepth, dia, included);
  }
  return cut;
}

/// A taper from diameter `top` at the mouth, narrowing at `included` radians
/// and running to nothing. The general form chamfer and countersink are
/// special cases of.
taper(top, included) {
  cut(zTop) {
    return coneVoid(zTop, top, included);
  }
  return cut;
}

/// A taper from diameter `top` at the mouth, narrowing at `included` radians,
/// with a flat floor `depth` below the mouth — a frustum rather than a cone
/// run to nothing.
taper(top, included, depth) {
  cut(zTop) {
    return max(coneVoid(zTop, top, included), (zTop - depth) - p.z);
  }
  return cut;
}

/// An ANSI B94.11 center: a pilot drill of diameter `pilotDia` to `depth`,
/// under a 60-degree countersink out to `csDia`. The feature a shaft end
/// carries so it can be turned between centers — the likeliest occupant of
/// the two holes at the ends of a shaft.
centerHole(pilotDia, csDia, depth) {
  cut(zTop) {
    return min(drillVoid(zTop, pilotDia, depth, pointAngle118),
               coneVoid(zTop, csDia, centerAngle60));
  }
  return cut;
}

// =========================================================
// Non-Round Sections
// =========================================================

/// A hexagonal socket `acrossFlats` wide, through. Flats face +-x.
hexSocket(acrossFlats) {
  cut(zTop) {
    return hexXY(acrossFlats);
  }
  return cut;
}

/// A hexagonal socket `acrossFlats` wide, `depth` deep with a flat bottom.
/// Broached hex, a hex drive, a nut pocket.
hexSocket(acrossFlats, depth) {
  cut(zTop) {
    return prismVoid(zTop, hexXY(acrossFlats), depth);
  }
  return cut;
}

/// A square socket `acrossFlats` wide, through.
squareSocket(acrossFlats) {
  cut(zTop) {
    return squareXY(acrossFlats);
  }
  return cut;
}

/// A square socket `acrossFlats` wide, `depth` deep with a flat bottom.
squareSocket(acrossFlats, depth) {
  cut(zTop) {
    return prismVoid(zTop, squareXY(acrossFlats), depth);
  }
  return cut;
}

/// A TAPERED square socket, `acrossFlats` at the face, `depth` deep, each wall
/// leaning in at `taper` radians.
///
/// A wall taper wedges a tapered bit in and still lets it go under a straight
/// pull, which is how a Robertson is usually described. It is not how Robertson
/// drew it — his walls are parallel and the taper is the pyramid at the bottom —
/// and a modern recess holds its bit without one. For the recess itself use
/// drill.robertsonAt.
squareSocket(acrossFlats, depth, taper) {
  cut(zTop) {
    return squareTaperVoid(zTop, acrossFlats / 2.0, taper, depth);
  }
  return cut;
}

/// A TORX (hexalobular) socket cut clean through: `major` across the lobes,
/// `minor` across the flutes, at the drawn fillet proportion.
torx(major, minor) {
  cut(zTop) {
    return torxXY(major, minor);
  }
  return cut;
}

/// A TORX (hexalobular, ISO 10664) socket, `depth` deep with a flat bottom.
/// `major` is the diameter across the lobes — the A dimension a Torx size is
/// quoted by — and `minor` the diameter across the flutes between them.
///
/// Six lobes, always: hexalobular is what the standard calls it, and the lobe
/// count is not a parameter of the standard. The flanks are straight and radial
/// rather than tapered, which is the entire reason a Torx bit does not cam out
/// and does not need to be pressed into the screw to stay there.
///
/// Sizes come from the catalog: catalog.torxMajor(25) and catalog.torxMinor(25)
/// are a T25.
torx(major, minor, depth) {
  cut(zTop) {
    return prismVoid(zTop, torxXY(major, minor), depth);
  }
  return cut;
}

/// A Torx socket with its fillet radius given rather than proportioned.
torx(major, minor, depth, filletR) {
  cut(zTop) {
    return prismVoid(zTop, torxXY(major, minor, filletR), depth);
  }
  return cut;
}

// =========================================================
// Drives as Values
// =========================================================
//
// The factories above return a feature closure, which is what drill.hole folds.
// A screw head is not a hole: its drive is one recess at a plane the head knows
// and the caller does not, so fastener wants the same shapes as plain values.
// These are that, and they are the same bodies — there is no second copy of any
// of this geometry.

/// A hexagonal socket `acrossFlats` wide and `depth` deep, its face at `zTop`.
hexSocketAt(zTop, acrossFlats, depth) {
  return prismVoid(zTop, hexXY(acrossFlats), depth);
}

/// A square socket `acrossFlats` wide and `depth` deep, its face at `zTop`,
/// with straight walls.
squareSocketAt(zTop, acrossFlats, depth) {
  return prismVoid(zTop, squareXY(acrossFlats), depth);
}

/// A TAPERED square socket, `acrossFlats` at the face and leaning in at
/// `taper` radians per wall — see drill.squareSocket.
squareSocketAt(zTop, acrossFlats, depth, taper) {
  return squareTaperVoid(zTop, acrossFlats / 2.0, taper, depth);
}

/// A ROBERTSON (square) recess `acrossFlats` wide with its face at `zTop`,
/// `depth` to its deepest point: parallel walls, and a 45-degree chamfer of
/// `leg` running out from the floor to them. A leg of 0 is a flat bottom; a leg
/// of half the width across flats is the four-sided pyramid of the 1909 patent.
/// Sizes come from the catalog:
///
///   drill.robertsonAt(zTop, catalog.robertsonAcross(2.0),
///                     catalog.robertsonDepth(2.0), 0.5)
robertsonAt(zTop, acrossFlats, depth, leg) {
  return robertsonVoid(zTop, acrossFlats / 2.0, depth, leg);
}

/// A Robertson recess with the patent's pyramid bottom — the chamfer taken all
/// the way to the axis.
robertsonAt(zTop, acrossFlats, depth) {
  return robertsonVoid(zTop, acrossFlats / 2.0, depth, acrossFlats / 2.0);
}

/// An ISO 4757 TYPE Z (POZIDRIV) recess seated on the reference plane `zRef`,
/// `dia` across there — the screw's m — in the form of one recess number:
/// core b, wing f, knee g, valley lean alpha and delta, and mark depth j, from
/// catalog.pozidrivCore and its neighbours. The marks run out to the recess
/// diameter at 60 degrees across, the near end of ISO's "1 to 1,25 m" and the
/// narrowest of its "60 min, 90 max" — a reading, not a dimension.
///
/// fastener.panPozidrivDrive sizes one from the catalog.
pozidrivAt(zRef, dia, core, wing, knee, lean, delta, markDepth) {
  return pozidrivVoid(zRef, dia, core, wing, knee, lean, delta, markDepth,
                      dia / 2.0, 1.04719755);
}

/// The ISO 4757 type Z GAUGE — a Pozidriv screwdriver's point — as a SOLID,
/// its knee at `zKnee` and cut off at `zTop`; its point is `point` below the
/// knee. Pass Table 4's numbers (catalog.pozidrivGaugeCore and neighbours).
pozidrivGauge(zKnee, zTop, core, wing, knee, point, lean, delta) {
  return pozidrivGaugeSolid(zKnee, zTop, core, wing, knee, point, lean, delta);
}

/// An ISO 4757 TYPE H (PHILLIPS) recess seated on the reference plane `zRef`,
/// `dia` across there — the m of the screw's product standard — in the form of
/// one recess number: core width b, ridge facet e and angle alpha, point
/// diameter g and ridge lean beta, from catalog.phillipsCore and its
/// neighbours. `root` is No. 0's ridge radius and zero for every other number.
///
/// The wings are what the four ribs leave between them, so their thickness f
/// is not passed; ISO's Table 1 f comes back out of b, e and the angles. The
/// depth is not passed either: the wing-end cone crosses zRef at `dia`, which
/// sinks the one form of this number as deep as the screw's m puts it.
///
///   drill.phillipsAt(2.716, 4.4, 1.47, 0.815, 2.29, radians(140.0), radians(5.75), 0.0)
phillipsAt(zRef, dia, core, facet, g, ridge, lean, root) {
  return phillipsVoid(zRef, dia, core, facet, g, ridge, lean, root);
}

/// The ISO 4757 type H GAUGE — the point of a Phillips screwdriver — as a
/// SOLID, its flat end at `zTip` and cut off at `zTop`: the one solid in this
/// file, here because it is the recess's own construction read from the other
/// side. Its blades are what the same four ribs leave standing inside the 26
/// degree 30 cone, which is g across where it meets an 18 degree end chamfer
/// running in to a flat `flat` across. Pass Table 2's numbers
/// (catalog.phillipsGaugeCore and its neighbours), not the recess's.
phillipsGauge(zTip, zTop, core, facet, g, flat, ridge, lean, root) {
  we = 0.46251225;
  ch = 0.31415927;
  r = length(p.xy);
  zg = zTip + ((g - flat) / 2.0) * tan(ch);
  zb = zTip + ((core - flat) / 2.0) * tan(ch);
  // The blades run out along the axes to the wing-end cone, widest at the top.
  reach = g / 2.0 + (zTop - zg) * tan(we);
  form = max(r * cos(we) - (p.z - zg) * sin(we) - (g / 2.0) * cos(we),
             zTip - p.z,
             (zTip - p.z) * cos(ch) + (r - flat / 2.0) * sin(ch),
             p.z - zTop,
             neg(phillipsRibs(core, facet, ridge, lean, root, zb)));
  form.box = [[-reach, -reach, zTip], [reach, reach, zTop]];
  return form;
}

/// A cross recess with its face at `zTop` — see drill.crossRecess.
crossRecessAt(zTop, dia, wingWidth, depth, flank) {
  return crossVoid(zTop, wingWidth / 2.0, dia / 2.0, depth, flank);
}

/// A cross recess with a plan corner fillet of radius `rootR`, its face at
/// `zTop` — see drill.crossRecess.
crossRecessAt(zTop, dia, wingWidth, depth, flank, rootR) {
  return crossRootVoid(zTop, wingWidth / 2.0, dia / 2.0, depth, flank, rootR);
}

/// A cross recess with a plan corner fillet AND a conical outer trim leaning
/// out at `outerTaper`, its face at `zTop` — see drill.crossRecess. `dia` is
/// then the recess diameter AT THE FACE, which is where it is measured.
crossRecessAt(zTop, dia, wingWidth, depth, flank, rootR, outerTaper) {
  return crossRootVoid(zTop, wingWidth / 2.0, dia / 2.0, depth, flank, rootR,
                       outerTaper);
}

/// A ribbed cross recess with its face at `zTop`, ribs running out to the full
/// recess diameter — see drill.crossRecessRibbed.
crossRecessRibbedAt(zTop, dia, wingWidth, depth, flank,
                    ribWidth, ribDepth, ribFlank) {
  return crossRibbedVoid(zTop, wingWidth / 2.0, dia / 2.0, depth, flank,
                         ribWidth / 2.0, ribFlank, ribDepth, dia / 2.0);
}

/// A ribbed cross recess with its face at `zTop` — see drill.crossRecessRibbed.
crossRecessRibbedAt(zTop, dia, wingWidth, depth, flank,
                    ribDia, ribWidth, ribDepth, ribFlank) {
  return crossRibbedVoid(zTop, wingWidth / 2.0, dia / 2.0, depth, flank,
                         ribWidth / 2.0, ribFlank, ribDepth, ribDia / 2.0);
}

/// A ribbed cross recess with a plan corner fillet of radius `rootR`, its face
/// at `zTop` — see drill.crossRecessRibbed.
crossRecessRibbedAt(zTop, dia, wingWidth, depth, flank,
                    ribDia, ribWidth, ribDepth, ribFlank, rootR) {
  return crossRibbedRootVoid(zTop, wingWidth / 2.0, dia / 2.0, depth, flank,
                             ribWidth / 2.0, ribFlank, ribDepth, ribDia / 2.0,
                             rootR);
}

/// A ribbed cross recess with a plan corner fillet AND a conical outer trim
/// leaning out at `outerTaper`, its face at `zTop`.
crossRecessRibbedAt(zTop, dia, wingWidth, depth, flank,
                    ribDia, ribWidth, ribDepth, ribFlank, rootR, outerTaper) {
  return crossRibbedRootVoid(zTop, wingWidth / 2.0, dia / 2.0, depth, flank,
                             ribWidth / 2.0, ribFlank, ribDepth, ribDia / 2.0,
                             rootR, outerTaper);
}

/// A TORX (hexalobular, ISO 10664) socket `depth` deep, its face at `zTop`:
/// `major` across the lobes, `minor` across the flutes. See drill.torx.
torxAt(zTop, major, minor, depth) {
  return prismVoid(zTop, torxXY(major, minor), depth);
}

/// A Torx socket with the fillet radius given — see drill.torx.
torxAt(zTop, major, minor, depth, filletR) {
  return prismVoid(zTop, torxXY(major, minor, filletR), depth);
}

/// A straight SLOT drive `width` across and `depth` deep, its face at `zTop`,
/// running clean across the head. The screwdriver that predates all the rest.
slotDriveAt(zTop, width, depth) {
  return prismVoid(zTop, abs(p.y) - width / 2.0, depth);
}

// =========================================================
// Identification Marks
// =========================================================
//
// A Phillips and a Pozidriv recess are near enough identical to look at, and
// driving one with the other's bit is what rounds the recess out. So the HEAD
// is marked rather than the recess: Pozidriv gets four lines stamped at 45
// degrees to its arms, and Phillips nothing at all.
//
// A JIS cross is not a third recess to mark. JIS B 1012, ASME B18.6.3's Type I
// and ISO 4757's type H are one recess for Nos. 1 to 4 — the ASME and ISO
// gauges agree dimension for dimension — and the single dot said to identify a
// JIS screw is in none of the standards read for this library.
//
// REFERENCES — the standards read: JIS B 1012:1985
// (https://kikakurui.com/b1/B1012-1985-01.html), JIS B 4633:1998, the
// screwdriver (https://kikakurui.com/b4/B4633-1998-01.html), ISO 4757:1983
// (https://cdn.standards.iteh.ai/samples/10742/d878fa0371e041b39de75de4d4426928/ISO-4757-1983.pdf)
// and ASME B18.6.3-2010 (unofficial copy,
// https://pdfcoffee.com/asme-b1863-2010-pdf-free.html). Secondary sources
// read on JIS against Phillips: https://www.tekton.com/blog/jis-vs-phillips-screwdriver-tip-geometry-and-fastener-compatibility,
// https://www.hanshin-neji.com/en/content/5486/phillips-vs-jis-drive-screw,
// https://www.abbeon.com/ItemFiles/articles/JISInfoSheet.pdf.
//
// These are shallow voids like any other, and a mark is not a drive: a head can
// carry both, and which one it carries is a fact about the standard rather than
// about the geometry.

/// The section of four TICK MARKS at 45 degrees to the axes, `width` across and
/// running from radius `innerR` out to `outerR`. Unbounded in z, like every
/// other section here — bound it with tickMarksAt for a flat face or engrave
/// for a curved one.
tickSection(width, innerR, outerR) {
  u = (p.x + p.y) * 0.70710678;
  v = (p.x - p.y) * 0.70710678;
  alongU = max(abs(v) - width / 2.0, abs(u) - outerR, innerR - abs(u));
  alongV = max(abs(u) - width / 2.0, abs(v) - outerR, innerR - abs(v));
  return min(alongU, alongV);
}

/// The section of a round DIMPLE of diameter `dia`, its centre `atR` out from
/// the axis along +X.
dimpleSection(dia, atR) {
  return length([p.x - atR, p.y]) - dia / 2.0;
}

/// Four shallow tick marks stamped `depth` into the FLAT face at `zTop`.
tickMarksAt(zTop, width, innerR, outerR, depth) {
  return prismVoid(zTop, tickSection(width, innerR, outerR), depth);
}

/// A shallow dimple `depth` into the FLAT face at `zTop`.
dimpleAt(zTop, dia, atR, depth) {
  return prismVoid(zTop, dimpleSection(dia, atR), depth);
}

/// ENGRAVE `section` into `surface`, `depth` deep, FOLLOWING THE SURFACE
/// rather than cutting down from a plane.
///
/// A mark bounded by a plane only works on a flat face. Stamp one on a pan
/// head — which is domed — and at the rim the head has already fallen below
/// that plane, so the mark cuts nothing and silently is not there. Offsetting
/// the surface itself instead works on any shape: the void is the part of the
/// section lying within `depth` of the surface, which is what a stamp does.
///
/// The void is OPEN ABOVE and bounded below by the plane z = `zFrom`.
///
/// Both of those are load-bearing, and each fixes a different way of getting
/// this wrong.
///
/// BOUNDED BELOW, because a surface offset follows EVERY surface the shape has.
/// Written as max(section, -(surface + depth)) alone, the void is every point of
/// the section not deeper than `depth` inside — which is the whole of space
/// outside the part as well. A tick mark written that way does not mark a head;
/// it saws the screw in half down four planes. `zFrom` picks the face the mark
/// belongs on, which for a screw head is the top.
///
/// OPEN ABOVE, and NOT closed off with the surface itself. Adding `surface` to
/// the max does bound the void — and it bounds it with the crown, putting the
/// void's top face exactly on the face it opens onto. Then at the crown the
/// field reads 0 from both sides: a membrane, not an opening. It samples
/// correctly at every depth inside the mark and RENDERS AS NO MARK AT ALL,
/// which is the defect this file's header warns about, arrived at from the
/// other direction. The void runs up into the air instead and the head's own
/// crown is what bounds it.
///
///   head = fastener.panHead(12.0, 4.4);
///   mark = drill.engrave(head, drill.tickSection(1.0, 3.0, 5.4), 0.45, 2.2);
engrave(surface, section, depth, zFrom) {
  return max(section, -(surface + depth), zFrom - p.z);
}

/// A CROSS RECESS: four wings of width `wingWidth` reaching out to diameter
/// `dia`, cut `depth` deep, their flanks tapering at `flank` radians from the
/// axis. A GENERIC tapered cross — four slabs — and NOT ISO 4757's type H,
/// whose wings are what four ribs leave between them; that one is
/// drill.phillipsAt.
///
/// THE FLANK ANGLE decides whether a driver cams out. Taper the flanks and a
/// driver rides up out of the recess under torque — which is deliberate on a
/// Phillips: cam-out was a torque limiter before torque drivers were cheap.
/// Bring them near parallel and it stops, which is half of what a Pozidriv
/// does differently.
///
///   drill.crossRecess(3.5, 1.8, 2.0, radians(7.0))
///
/// This is the shape of a recess, not a bit seat: the wing ends are cut by
/// the outer diameter rather than tapering in plan, and the bottom is flat
/// rather than pointed. Enough to draw a cross in a head and to prove a driver
/// clears it; not enough to model a driver seating.
crossRecess(dia, wingWidth, depth, flank) {
  cut(zTop) {
    return crossVoid(zTop, wingWidth / 2.0, dia / 2.0, depth, flank);
  }
  return cut;
}

/// A cross recess with a PLAN CORNER FILLET of radius `rootR` where adjacent
/// wings meet.
///
/// Rounded, the arms of a Phillips appear to flow into one another; left
/// sharp, a cross reads as four straight slots crossing.
///
/// It is a plan-view radius, in the section you would see looking down the
/// axis, and NOT a break around the mouth.
crossRecess(dia, wingWidth, depth, flank, rootR) {
  cut(zTop) {
    return crossRootVoid(zTop, wingWidth / 2.0, dia / 2.0, depth, flank, rootR);
  }
  return cut;
}

/// A cross recess WITH RIBS: the four wings above plus four secondary ribs at
/// 45 degrees between them, `ribWidth` across and `ribDepth` deep. The
/// Pozidriv and Supadriv family, and the four tick marks stamped between the
/// arms on such a screw head are exactly these.
///
/// The ribs are why it does not cam out: they give the bit a second set of
/// faces to bear on that are not part of the tapered wing. Supadriv differs
/// from Pozidriv in the rib shape rather than in having them, so both are this
/// feature at different numbers.
///
/// A POZIDRIV'S WINGS ARE VERY NEARLY PARALLEL. Pass a Phillips flank angle
/// here and the result is not a Pozidriv with ribs added; it is a Phillips with
/// ribs added, which cams out exactly as a Phillips does and so has no reason
/// to carry ribs at all. Not camming out IS the design: the ribs give the bit
/// somewhere to bear, and the near-vertical flanks are what stop it climbing.
///
/// The ribs are shorter than the wings — `ribDia` rather than `dia` — as well
/// as narrower and shallower.
crossRecessRibbed(dia, wingWidth, depth, flank,
                  ribDia, ribWidth, ribDepth, ribFlank) {
  cut(zTop) {
    return crossRibbedVoid(zTop, wingWidth / 2.0, dia / 2.0, depth, flank,
                           ribWidth / 2.0, ribFlank, ribDepth, ribDia / 2.0);
  }
  return cut;
}

/// A ribbed cross recess whose ribs run out to the full recess diameter.
///
/// The older form, kept because it is a shape somebody may want and because
/// giving the ribs their own length was an addition rather than a correction of
/// this. For a Pozidriv pass ribDia explicitly and make it smaller than dia.
crossRecessRibbed(dia, wingWidth, depth, flank, ribWidth, ribDepth, ribFlank) {
  cut(zTop) {
    return crossRibbedVoid(zTop, wingWidth / 2.0, dia / 2.0, depth, flank,
                           ribWidth / 2.0, ribFlank, ribDepth, dia / 2.0);
  }
  return cut;
}

/// A ribbed cross recess with a plan corner fillet of radius `rootR`.
crossRecessRibbed(dia, wingWidth, depth, flank,
                  ribDia, ribWidth, ribDepth, ribFlank, rootR) {
  cut(zTop) {
    return crossRibbedRootVoid(zTop, wingWidth / 2.0, dia / 2.0, depth, flank,
                               ribWidth / 2.0, ribFlank, ribDepth, ribDia / 2.0,
                               rootR);
  }
  return cut;
}

/// A D-hole through: a bore of diameter `dia` with one flat, `acrossFlat`
/// measured from the flat to the far side of the bore. The internal
/// counterpart to a D-shaft, and the usual anti-rotation feature for a knob
/// or a light-duty drive. The flat faces +x.
dFlat(dia, acrossFlat) {
  cut(zTop) {
    return max(cylinderZ(vector.zero, dia / 2.0), p.x - (acrossFlat - dia / 2.0));
  }
  return cut;
}

/// A D-hole `depth` deep with a flat bottom.
dFlat(dia, acrossFlat, depth) {
  cut(zTop) {
    section = max(cylinderZ(vector.zero, dia / 2.0), p.x - (acrossFlat - dia / 2.0));
    return prismVoid(zTop, section, depth);
  }
  return cut;
}

/// A double-D hole through: a bore of diameter `dia` with two opposed flats
/// `acrossFlats` apart, facing +-x.
doubleD(dia, acrossFlats) {
  cut(zTop) {
    return max(cylinderZ(vector.zero, dia / 2.0), abs(p.x) - acrossFlats / 2.0);
  }
  return cut;
}

/// A double-D hole `depth` deep with a flat bottom.
doubleD(dia, acrossFlats, depth) {
  cut(zTop) {
    section = max(cylinderZ(vector.zero, dia / 2.0), abs(p.x) - acrossFlats / 2.0);
    return prismVoid(zTop, section, depth);
  }
  return cut;
}

/// The smooth void a tapped hole presents at its MAXIMUM MATERIAL CONDITION: a
/// cylinder at the thread's basic MINOR diameter, `depth` deep, for the coarse
/// pitch of this `major`. Threading is a machining operation that only ever
/// takes material from there, so the screw that goes in INTERFERES with this
/// bore by design, across the annulus out to the major diameter. Prove the fit
/// with the engagement zone that interference lives in — see thread.engaged —
/// and draw the thread with thread.femaleRelief on the part's material.
///
/// A drawing writes the drill and the thread as two lines ("DRILL 5.0 DEEP 20
/// / M6x1 DEEP 15"), and so do you: list a blind() under a tap(). A tap drill
/// wider than the basic minor is the bore then, which is how a tapped hole is
/// made; pass that bore to thread.femaleRelief as well.
tap(major, depth) {
  // Its own closure rather than a call to the pitched form: hole() folds a
  // feature it can see, and a closure handed back through another call is not
  // one. The pitch is found inside it, one call to a statement: a closure
  // cannot see the enclosing function's locals, and a parameter it captures
  // is lost as the argument of a call nested inside another call's arguments.
  cut(zTop) {
    pitchVal = thread.metricPitch(major);
    minorR = thread.minorDia(major, pitchVal) / 2.0;
    return max(cylinderZ(vector.zero, minorR), (zTop - depth) - p.z);
  }
  return cut;
}

/// tap() for any pitch — a FINE thread's minor diameter is larger than the
/// coarse one of the same major, so the pitch is part of the bore.
tap(major, pitchVal, depth) {
  cut(zTop) {
    return max(cylinderZ(vector.zero, thread.minorDia(major, pitchVal) / 2.0),
               (zTop - depth) - p.z);
  }
  return cut;
}

/// A tapped hole with a display-only thread of the given major diameter and
/// pitch, `depth` deep. The real ISO 68-1 internal form — wide crest flat,
/// narrow root — from the thread package, so this hole and the screw that goes
/// into it are drawn from one definition.
///
/// It is tap() with the thread cut into it, so prove against tap(): a
/// 60-degree flank makes an interval proof subdivide to thread scale for no
/// engineering information the smooth form does not already give.
tapThreaded(major, pitch, depth) {
  cut(zTop) {
    return max(thread.female(major, pitch), (zTop - depth) - p.z);
  }
  return cut;
}

/// A tapped hole whose thread is a SINUSOID rather than the standard V. Not a
/// real form, but it meshes and bounds more cheaply — reach for it when the
/// thread is scenery rather than the subject.
tapSin(major, pitch, depth) {
  cut(zTop) {
    return max(thread.femaleSin(major, pitch), (zTop - depth) - p.z);
  }
  return cut;
}

// =========================================================
// Located Features
// =========================================================

/// A relief groove of diameter `dia` between `fromDepth` and `toDepth` below
/// the mouth, toDepth the deeper. The one feature WIDER than the bore above
/// it, so the one that needs both bounds — an undercut with no top would eat
/// the wall it exists to relieve. A thread relief, a retaining-ring groove, an
/// O-ring gland of square section.
undercut(dia, fromDepth, toDepth) {
  cut(zTop) {
    return max(cylinderZ(vector.zero, dia / 2.0),
               (zTop - toDepth) - p.z, p.z - (zTop - fromDepth));
  }
  return cut;
}

/// A female straight-sided spline cut clean through: `count` grooves outward
/// to diameter `major`, each `width` across, bounded by neither face. The
/// grooves only — list a blind() at the minor diameter with it. count >= 3.
spline(count, major, width) {
  cut(zTop) {
    return splineVoid(count, major, width);
  }
  return cut;
}

/// A female straight-sided spline: `count` grooves cut outward to diameter
/// `major`, each `width` across, running from the mouth down to `toDepth`.
/// The DIN 5463 parallel-side form. The grooves only — list a blind() or
/// bore() at the minor diameter with it, so the bore reads as its own
/// line, the way a drawing writes it. count >= 3.
spline(count, major, width, toDepth) {
  cut(zTop) {
    return max(splineVoid(count, major, width), (zTop - toDepth) - p.z);
  }
  return cut;
}

/// A spline bounded at both ends, between `fromDepth` and `toDepth` — a set
/// of grooves that neither starts at the mouth nor runs out of it. Give
/// `fromDepth` 0 only if something wider above already opens the face:
/// a groove that stops exactly on the face has a surface lying in it.
splineBetween(count, major, width, fromDepth, toDepth) {
  cut(zTop) {
    return max(splineVoid(count, major, width),
               (zTop - toDepth) - p.z, p.z - (zTop - fromDepth));
  }
  return cut;
}

/// A keyway cut clean through the wall of a bore of diameter `boreDia`: one
/// slot `width` across, `depth` radially outward, bounded by neither face. On
/// +x. What a keyed bore in a plate or a hub wants.
keyway(width, depth, boreDia) {
  cut(zTop) {
    return keywayVoid(width, depth, boreDia);
  }
  return cut;
}

/// A keyway in the wall of a bore of diameter `boreDia`: one slot `width`
/// across, cut `depth` radially outward from the bore, running from the mouth
/// down to `toDepth`. On +x. Takes the bore diameter explicitly, because a
/// keyway is dimensioned on its own detail rather than stacked.
keyway(width, depth, boreDia, toDepth) {
  cut(zTop) {
    return max(keywayVoid(width, depth, boreDia), (zTop - toDepth) - p.z);
  }
  return cut;
}

/// A keyway bounded at both ends: an internal keyseat that neither starts at
/// the mouth nor runs out of it, between `fromDepth` and `toDepth`. Give
/// `fromDepth` 0 only if something wider above already opens the face.
keywayBetween(width, depth, boreDia, fromDepth, toDepth) {
  cut(zTop) {
    return max(keywayVoid(width, depth, boreDia),
               (zTop - toDepth) - p.z, p.z - (zTop - fromDepth));
  }
  return cut;
}

/// An obround slot through: diameter `dia`, end centers `centers` apart —
/// which is how a drawing dimensions one, not overall length — with the long
/// axis at `ang` radians from +x.
slot(dia, centers, ang) {
  cut(zTop) {
    return capsuleXY(dia, centers, ang);
  }
  return cut;
}

/// An obround slot `depth` deep with a flat bottom.
slot(dia, centers, ang, depth) {
  cut(zTop) {
    return prismVoid(zTop, capsuleXY(dia, centers, ang), depth);
  }
  return cut;
}

/// The chamfer for a slot: a 45-degree lead-in of leg `leg` that FOLLOWS the
/// obround it breaks, given that slot's own `dia`, `centers` and `ang`. List
/// it with the slot, the way chamfer() is listed with its bore.
///
/// A round chamfer on a slot breaks the edge at the two ends and cuts a
/// crescent out of the face along the straight, which is neither what the tool
/// does nor what the drawing says. The edge to break is the slot's, so the
/// chamfer has to be the slot's shape.
slotChamfer(dia, centers, ang, leg) {
  cut(zTop) {
    return chamferSection(zTop, capsuleXY(dia, centers, ang), leg);
  }
  return cut;
}

// =========================================================
// Placement
// =========================================================

/// The remap coordinate that puts a +Z-datum hole at `point` with its axis
/// along `axis` — the direction the drill enters, which is the outward normal
/// of the face it goes into. Pass it to remap:
///
///   remap(drill.hole([drill.thru(6.6)], 0), drill.axisAt(faceCenter, faceNormal))
///
/// THE IMPLEMENTATION IS map.axisAt, which is where it belongs: aligning a
/// datum to a direction is not a fact about holes, and map is where rotateAxis
/// and the pattern remaps already live. This forwards so a hole can be placed
/// without reaching outside the package it came from.
axisAt(point, axis) {
  return map.axisAt(point, axis);
}
